import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={gravitation:{theory:{concept:"[[The law of universal gravitation: any two bodies attract each other; for point masses or spheres the force is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres, F = G \xB7 M \xB7 m / r\xB2.]]"},section:{law:{title:"Newton's law of gravitation",text:"Isaac Newton published this law in 1687; the gravitational constant in it is G = 6.674\xB710\u207B\xB9\xB9 N\xB7m\xB2/kg\xB2. [[The same law makes an apple fall, keeps the Moon on its orbit and holds galaxies together.]] G is tiny, so the pull between everyday objects is far too weak to feel; only a body as massive as a planet makes it strong."},inverseSquare:{title:"Twice as far, a quarter of the pull",text:"[[The force falls with the square of the distance from the centre.]] One Earth radius above the surface (6371 km up) you are twice as far from the centre, so you weigh a quarter of your weight; at three times the distance, a ninth. The dashed circles mark these distances. Near the surface the change is small: on top of Everest (9 km up) you weigh only about 0.3 % less."},weight:{title:"Mass and weight",text:"Mass, measured in kilograms, tells how much matter a body has and how hard it is to speed up; it is the same everywhere. [[Weight is the force with which a body at rest presses on its support or pulls on its hanger; on a planet\u2019s surface it equals the planet\u2019s pull on the body, W = m \xB7 g, where g = G \xB7 M / r\xB2 is the gravitational field strength.]] On the Earth g \u2248 9.8 N/kg, so a 60 kg person weighs about 590 N. On the Moon the same person weighs about 97 N \u2014 six times less \u2014 but has the same mass."},planets:{title:"Different worlds",text:"The field strength at the surface, g = G \xB7 M / R\xB2, depends on both the mass and the size of the planet. The Moon is small and light: g = 1.6 m/s\xB2. Mars: 3.7 m/s\xB2. Jupiter is 318 times more massive than the Earth, but also 11 times wider, so its pull at the cloud tops is 'only' 2.5 times the Earth's: 24.8 m/s\xB2."},mutual:{title:"The pull goes both ways",text:"By Newton's third law, a falling apple pulls the Earth with exactly the same force as the Earth pulls the apple. The Earth does not seem to move only because its mass is 6\xB710\xB2\u2074 kg: the same force gives it an acceleration about 6\xB710\xB2\u2075 times smaller than that of a 100 g apple. Astronauts on the space station are not weightless because gravity is missing \u2014 at 400 km it is still 89 % of the surface value \u2014 but because they fall around the Earth together with the station."},cavendish:{title:"Weighing the Earth",text:"Newton could not measure G. In 1798 Henry Cavendish did it with a torsion balance: two small lead balls on a rod hung on a thin wire, and two large lead balls placed beside them twisted the wire by a tiny angle. From the twist he found the attraction between the balls \u2014 and, knowing g, the mass of the whole Earth. That is why his experiment is often called 'weighing the Earth'. Measuring G precisely is still hard today: it is the least precisely known of the fundamental constants."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"How much does a 60 kg person weigh on the Moon? (About 97 N.) What is their mass there? (Still 60 kg.) At what height above the Earth is the weight a quarter? (One Earth radius, 6371 km.) Does the Earth pull you harder than you pull the Earth? (No \u2014 the forces are equal.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{law:"Newton's law of gravitation between two masses at the distance r",weight:"Weight: the planet's pull on a body of mass m",field:"Gravitational field strength at distance r from the centre",inverse:"Inverse square: at twice the distance the force is a quarter",constant:"The gravitational constant, first measured by Cavendish",landing:"Landing speed after falling from rest at r\u2080 (energy conservation)"},variable:{force:"Gravitational force (weight)",planetMass:"Mass of the planet",mass:"Mass of the body",distance:"Distance from the planet's centre",radius:"Radius of the planet",field:"Gravitational field strength",constant:"Gravitational constant"},assumption:{sphere:"The planet is a perfect, non-rotating sphere whose mass acts as if it were all at its centre; real gravity varies slightly from place to place.",air:"There is no air: a real body falling through the atmosphere is slowed down a great deal.",straight:"The body falls straight down from rest; with a sideways speed it would go into orbit instead.",scale:"The planet is to scale with the distances, but the body is drawn far larger. The animation is sped up so that every fall lasts a few seconds; the times shown are the real ones."},param:{planet:"Planet",mass:"Mass of the body",height:"Height above the surface",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{mass:"The mass is the same on every planet; the weight is not.",height:"Run the simulation to let the body fall from this height."},planet:{earth:"Earth",moon:"Moon",mars:"Mars",jupiter:"Jupiter"},preset:{apple:"Apple",person:"Person",satellite:"Satellite",surface:"Surface",everest:"Everest",iss:"Space station",oneRadius:"One radius",geostationary:"Geostationary"},measure:{force:"Gravitational force (weight)",surfaceWeight:"Weight on the surface",weightShare:"Share of the surface weight",height:"Height",fieldStrength:"Field strength",distance:"Distance from the centre",distanceInRadii:"Distance in planet radii",fallTime:"Time to fall to the surface",landingSpeed:"Speed on reaching the surface"},view:{onPlanet:"on the planet: {force}",ring2:"2R: \xBC of the weight",ring3:"3R: \u2151 of the weight",body:"{mass} \xB7 {height} up",status:"t = {time} \xB7 v = {speed} \xB7 time \xD7{speedUp}",landed:"Landed after {time} at {speed}",ringsOption:"Distances 2R and 3R",reactionOption:"The pull on the planet"},graph:{forceDistance:"Force against distance from the centre",speed:"Speed of the falling body"},challenge:{moonHundred:{title:"100 newtons on the Moon",description:"Choose a mass that weighs 100 N on the Moon's surface.",hint:"On the Moon g = 1.62 m/s\xB2, so m = 100 / 1.62."},quarter:{title:"A quarter of the weight",description:"Find the height where a body weighs a quarter of its surface weight.",hint:"A quarter of the force means twice the distance from the centre."},jupiter:{title:"1000 newtons, 50 kilograms",description:"Make a body of at most 50 kg weigh 1000 N or more on a surface.",hint:"You need g \u2265 20 m/s\xB2. Which planet pulls that hard?"},moonGravity:{title:"Moon gravity above the Earth",description:"Find the height above the Earth where the field strength is the same as on the Moon's surface (1.62 m/s\xB2).",hint:"g falls as 1/r\xB2: you need r = R \xB7 \u221A(9.82 / 1.62), about 2.5 Earth radii from the centre."},fastLanding:{title:"2 km/s from a low height",description:"Drop a body onto the Earth from no higher than 300 km so that it lands at 2000 m/s or faster (no air).",hint:"Near the surface v \u2248 \u221A(2 \xB7 g \xB7 h); you need about 200 km."}}}},s={sim:a};export{s as default,a as sim};
